explain.narrative:
## Core concept
ANY (also called SOME in SQL) is used with a comparison operator and a subquery. The comparison is true when it is true for at least one value returned by the subquery. If the subquery is empty, ANY is false. NULL values can produce UNKNOWN when no comparison is true.

## Syntax
```sql
-- Dialect support varies; PostgreSQL and MySQL support quantified comparisons.
SELECT columns
FROM outer_table AS o
WHERE o.value > ANY (SELECT value FROM comparison_table);
```

## SQLite sample database
The following examples use a small fictional dataset. Each worked example starts with a repeatable setup script so it can be run independently in the SQL Playground.

## Worked examples
### 1. Find products priced above at least one target (EXISTS rewrite)
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS products;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS price_targets;
CREATE TABLE products (product_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, product_name TEXT NOT NULL, price NUMERIC NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE price_targets (target_price NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO products VALUES (1,'Notebook',40),(2,'Pen Set',15),(3,'Desk Lamp',120),(4,'Backpack',90);
INSERT INTO price_targets VALUES (20),(50),(100);
SELECT p.product_name, p.price FROM products AS p WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM price_targets AS t WHERE p.price > t.target_price) ORDER BY p.price;
```
Expected result: Notebook | 40; Backpack | 90; Desk Lamp | 120

For this non-NULL target set, EXISTS expresses the ANY comparison: at least one target is lower than the product price.

### 2. Find products cheaper than at least one target
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS products;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS price_targets;
CREATE TABLE products (product_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, product_name TEXT NOT NULL, price NUMERIC NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE price_targets (target_price NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO products VALUES (1,'Notebook',40),(2,'Pen Set',15),(3,'Desk Lamp',120),(4,'Backpack',90);
INSERT INTO price_targets VALUES (20),(50),(100);
SELECT p.product_name, p.price FROM products AS p WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM price_targets AS t WHERE p.price < t.target_price) ORDER BY p.price;
```
Expected result: Pen Set | 15; Notebook | 40; Backpack | 90

A product qualifies when its price is lower than one or more target prices.

### 3. Compare with any target using a scalar minimum
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS products;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS price_targets;
CREATE TABLE products (product_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, product_name TEXT NOT NULL, price NUMERIC NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE price_targets (target_price NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO products VALUES (1,'Notebook',40),(2,'Pen Set',15),(3,'Desk Lamp',120),(4,'Backpack',90);
INSERT INTO price_targets VALUES (20),(50),(100);
SELECT product_name, price FROM products WHERE price > (SELECT MIN(target_price) FROM price_targets) ORDER BY price;
```
Expected result: Notebook | 40; Backpack | 90; Desk Lamp | 120

For a non-empty set with no NULL values, greater than ANY is equivalent to greater than the minimum value.

### 4. Find products that match at least one exact target
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS products;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS price_targets;
CREATE TABLE products (product_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, product_name TEXT NOT NULL, price NUMERIC NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE price_targets (target_price NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO products VALUES (1,'Notebook',40),(2,'Pen Set',15),(3,'Desk Lamp',120),(4,'Backpack',90);
INSERT INTO price_targets VALUES (20),(50),(100);
SELECT p.product_name, p.price FROM products AS p WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM price_targets AS t WHERE p.price = t.target_price) ORDER BY p.price;
```
Expected result: No rows

No product price equals any target price, so the existence test returns no products.

### 5. Find products above at least one selected target
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS products;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS price_targets;
CREATE TABLE products (product_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, product_name TEXT NOT NULL, price NUMERIC NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE price_targets (target_price NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO products VALUES (1,'Notebook',40),(2,'Pen Set',15),(3,'Desk Lamp',120),(4,'Backpack',90);
INSERT INTO price_targets VALUES (20),(50),(100);
SELECT p.product_name FROM products AS p WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM price_targets AS t WHERE t.target_price >= 50 AND p.price > t.target_price) ORDER BY p.product_id;
```
Expected result: Desk Lamp; Backpack

The subquery can filter the comparison set before testing whether any comparison succeeds.

## Query behaviour and dialect notes
Quantified comparison syntax (ANY/SOME/ALL) is not supported by SQLite. The runnable SQLite exercises use logically equivalent EXISTS or NOT EXISTS rewrites over the supplied non-NULL values. PostgreSQL and MySQL support quantified comparisons; verify the exact feature and syntax against the target database version. Empty subqueries and NULL values require separate reasoning and are not interchangeable with simple MIN/MAX rewrites.

## Common mistakes
- Assuming SQLite accepts ANY/SOME/ALL quantified syntax.
- Treating ANY as ALL or confusing their truth conditions.
- Ignoring empty-subquery behaviour.
- Applying MIN/MAX rewrites when NULLs or empty sets change the semantics.
- Forgetting that a single counterexample defeats an ALL comparison.

## Best practices
Use explicit aliases and readable predicates. Test edge cases such as missing matches, duplicate values, empty sets and NULLs. Keep the database dialect visible whenever a feature is not portable.

